    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d781">

                <pb facs="0866" n="856" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg856"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Moscow Embassy Files: Lot F–96, Box 2</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Roosevelt to the Chairman of the Council of
                        Peopled Commissars of the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Stalin</hi>)<anchor n="34" xml:id="d781-f1"/>
                </head>
                <dateline>
                    <date ana="#date_undated-inferred-from-document-content" calendar="gregorian"
                        when="1944-04-04">April 4, 1944</date>
                </dateline>
                <p>Many thanks for your message of March 25.<anchor n="35" xml:id="d781-f2"/> It is
                    my hope that the International Labor Organization at the coming meeting will <pb
                        facs="0867" n="857" xml:id="pg857"/> make it clear that it no longer is an
                    organ of the League of Nations and that it will affiliate itself with the United
                    Nations. I trust, therefore, that the Soviet Union will have representatives at
                    the next conference.</p>
                <p>I will keep you informed of what takes place at the meeting in Philadelphia.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d781-f1" n="34" type="source" xml:id="d781-f1-content">
                <p>Paraphrase received through Naval channels at the Embassy in Moscow on April 3,
                    1944, and sent on for Stalin in a letter to Molotov on the next day.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d781-f2" n="35" xml:id="d781-f2-content">
                <p>For text, see Ministry of Foreign Affairs of the U.S.S.R.: <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Correspondence Between the Chairman of the Council of
                        Ministers of the U.S.S.R. and the Presidents of the U.S.A. and the Prime
                        Ministers of Great Britain During the Great Patriotic War of 1941–1945</hi>,
                    vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 136.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
